/nemotron-3.5-super-vl-120b-a12b","response_format":{"type":"json_object"},"temperature":0},"response":{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"logprobs":null,"message":{"content":"{\"items\": [{\"hypothesis_id\": \"AND(VELOCITY_OFFSET,SPEED_STEP)\", \"rationale\": \"The s2_summary shows this structure achieves the lowest RMSE (0.014674) among all passing models, indicating the best fit to the observed position deviations without overfitting. The consistent positive offset in observed_x relative to nominal_x across all time steps supports a velocity offset, while the gradually increasing deviation magnitude suggests a speed step. The command_feedback entry confirms a missed execution at step 37, consistent with timing irregularities from these motion faults.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"obs:0:1.200\", \"obs:775:4.605\", \"command_feedback:0:37\"]}, {\"hypothesis_id\": \"AND(SENSOR_BIAS,VELOCITY_OFFSET,SPEED_STEP)\", \"rationale\": \"This structure also achieves the lowest RMSE (0.014674), matching the two-factor model. The persistent offset across all observations could reflect a sensor bias component in addition to the velocity offset and speed step. Including sensor bias accounts for any constant measurement offset while preserving the motion dynamics captured by the other factors.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"obs:0:1.200\", \"obs:775:4.605\"]}, {\"hypothesis_id\": \"VELOCITY_OFFSET\", \"rationale\": \"This single-factor model achieves a very low RMSE (0.014787), nearly matching the best multi-factor models. The steady offset pattern in observed positions relative to nominal positions is consistent with a constant velocity offset. It is the simplest structure that explains the majority of the deviation, making it a strong candidate under parsimony.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"obs:0:1.200\", \"obs:775:4.605\"]}, {\"hypothesis_id\": \"AND(VELOCITY_OFFSET,SPEED_STEP,ACTUATOR_DELAY)\", \"rationale\": \"This three-factor model achieves RMSE 0.014682, nearly identical to the best two-factor model. The actuator delay component may explain the missed command at step 37 and the execution_time of 3.934s exceeding the issued time of 3.7s. Combined with velocity offset and speed step, it captures both the steady motion deviation and the transient command execution anomaly.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"obs:0:1.200\", \"obs:775:4.605\", \"command_feedback:0:37\"]}, {\"hypothesis_id\": \"AND(SENSOR_BIAS,VELOCITY_OFFSET)\", \"rationale\": \"This model achieves RMSE 0.014738, very close to the best. The sensor bias term accounts for any constant measurement offset, while the velocity offset explains the systematic drift in observed positions. This is a reasonable alternative if the constant offset is attributed partly to sensor bias rather than purely to motion dynamics.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"obs:0:1.200\", \"obs:775:4.605\"]}, {\"hypothesis_id\": \"AND(VELOCITY_OFFSET,ACTUATOR_DELAY)\", \"rationale\": \"With RMSE 0.014795, this model performs nearly as well as the top candidates. The velocity offset explains the systematic position deviation, and the actuator delay accounts for the missed command execution at step 37. This combination is plausible if the primary issue is motion speed with occasional actuation lag.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"obs:0:1.200\", \"obs:775:4.605\", \"command_feedback:0:37\"]}, {\"hypothesis_id\": \"AND(SENSOR_BIAS,VELOCITY_OFFSET,ACTUATOR_DELAY)\", \"rationale\": \"This model achieves RMSE 0.014746, competitive with the best. It combines sensor bias, velocity offset, and actuator delay, covering constant measurement offset, systematic motion deviation, and command execution lag. The missed command at step 37 supports the actuator delay component.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"obs:0:1.200\", \"obs:775:4.605\", \"command_feedback:0:37\"]}, {\"hypothesis_id\": \"AND(SENSOR_BIAS,SPEED_STEP)\", \"rationale\": \"This model has a higher RMSE (0.019419) than the velocity-offset-containing models, suggesting speed step alone without velocity offset is less explanatory. However, it still passes and may be relevant if the deviation is primarily due to a speed change with a constant sensor bias, though the steady offset pattern argues against this being the best fit.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"obs:0:1.200\", \"obs:775:4.605\"]}, {\"hypothesis_id\": \"AND(SENSOR_BIAS,SPEED_STEP,ACTUATOR_DELAY)\", \"rationale\": \"With RMSE 0.019424, this is the highest among passing models, indicating it is the least supported. The absence of velocity offset weakens its ability to explain the consistent position drift. It is included only as a lower-priority alternative combining speed step, sensor bias, and actuator delay.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"obs:0:1.200\", \"obs:775:4.605\", \"command_feedback:0:37\"]}]}","reasoning_content":null,"role":"assistant"}}],"created":1792941526,"id":"chatcmpl-c71719bc-0766-4ecf-bc22-e3344f59d8e6","model":"private/nvidia/nemotron-3.5-super-120b-a12b","object":"chat.completion","service_tier":null,"system_fingerprint":null,"usage":{"completion_tokens":1221,"completion_tokens_details":{"accepted_prediction_tokens":null,"audio_tokens":null,"reasoning_tokens":0,"rejected_prediction_tokens":null},"prompt_tokens":9731,"prompt_tokens_details":{"audio_tokens":null,"cached_tokens":0},"total_tokens":10952}}}
